+// CONFINEMENT — buoyancy is ARCHIMEDES, so it needs a denser medium to rise through. Magma climbs the crust
+// because it is lighter than rock; it is the DENSEST thing in a seawater or air cell and cannot climb there at
+// all. This kernel's only gate used to be `the cell above is open`, which is exactly the case where the rule
+// does not hold: the moment a vent's overpressure reached open water it kept lifting, one cell per step, and
+// the quench froze what it lifted. That is the seabed spire — a mass elevator running on inverted buoyancy,
+// and no supply routing or height cap downstream could reach it because the lift happened here.
+//
+// The medium is now read from what is actually in the cell above: a CONNECTED MAGMA COLUMN transmits pressure
+// (a conduit really does push magma up through itself), open water or air does not. Emerging lava therefore
+// has to spread and descend under gravity like any other dense fluid, and a pile that wants to get taller has
+// to get wider first. Nothing here limits height — a conduit that stays full still climbs.
